	"name": "Merge Sort",
	"summary": "Splits the array into halves, sorts each half recursively and merges the sorted halves back together.",
	"description": [
		"Merge Sort is a divide and conquer algorithm. It splits the array into two halves, sorts each half with a recursive call and then merges the two sorted halves into one sorted part. A part with at most one element is already sorted, which is where the recursion stops.",
		"The real work happens in merge: both halves are copied aside and two indices, i and j, walk through them. In every step the smaller of the two front elements is written back into the array at index k. When one half is used up, the rest of the other half is copied over.",
		"The array can be halved about log₂ n times, and every level of the recursion merges n elements in total, so Merge Sort needs O(n log n) time in every case. The price is O(n) extra memory for the copied halves. It is stable, which is why many standard libraries use a variant of it to sort objects."
	],
